00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:11.830
but I definitely enjoyed it as a kid.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.050
- I played the game.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.070
I don't know which ones I played,

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:14.903
but I remember that.
- Oh, I didn't get that far.

00:08:14.903 --> 00:08:17.100
- I played like that Smash Bros Knockoff.

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.689
That was pretty fun I think.

00:08:18.689 --> 00:08:19.522
- There was a Smash Bros Knockoff?

00:08:19.522 --> 00:08:21.130
- Yeah, "Digimon: Rumble
Arena" it was called.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.580
They made like three of them.

00:08:24.222 --> 00:08:25.055
- Were you aware of that?

00:08:25.055 --> 00:08:26.030
- No, I wasn't aware of that.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:27.066
The only thing I'm aware of guys

00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:28.884
is just the theme tune stuck in my head.

00:08:28.884 --> 00:08:30.135
(Gigg laughs)

00:08:30.135 --> 00:08:30.968
- It's not even the good one,

00:08:30.968 --> 00:08:32.140
I know, it's the English one.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.150
At least like--`

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.110
- Bro, the English one's the
good one, what can I say?

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:39.060
- If you listen to the Japanese
opening, it's fucking epic.

00:08:40.555 --> 00:08:42.649
It's like the, you probably
heard it, it's like the,

00:08:42.649 --> 00:08:46.097
(Joey singing in foreign language)

00:08:46.097 --> 00:08:47.180
- Oh, that's the song I hear at karaoke!

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.481
(Joey singing in foreign language)

00:08:48.481 --> 00:08:49.964
- So it's completely different.

00:08:49.964 --> 00:08:52.167
♪ Digimon, digital monsters. ♪

00:08:52.167 --> 00:08:54.240
- No, they pulled a "Dragon Ball Z".

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.130
Dragon Ball Z Japanese opening
was like actual proper songs,

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:58.406
and the English one was just.

00:08:58.406 --> 00:09:00.157
♪ Jerk it, jerk it, move, jerk it. ♪

00:09:00.157 --> 00:09:01.623
It was like--

00:09:01.623 --> 00:09:03.070
- Boy, that was hype though,

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:04.010
- Come on, come on!

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:05.380
- It was a hype opening but I just love

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:06.213
how it's like, all right.

00:09:06.213 --> 00:09:07.808
We got a show called "Dragon Ball Z."

00:09:07.808 --> 00:09:09.960
(others laughing)

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.790
How many times can we see
the title of the story

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:12.623
in the song?

00:09:12.623 --> 00:09:14.567
- Bro, that's like big brain mode though.

00:09:14.567 --> 00:09:15.400
(laughs)

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.330
- I think the only other
lyric is come get me.

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:18.972
- Yeah exactly, right!

00:09:18.972 --> 00:09:20.889
- In the "Dragon Ball" opening?

00:09:20.889 --> 00:09:21.980
- I do not remember this at all.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:23.870
- You don't remember the
English "Dragon Ball" opening?

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:26.010
- No, I think it's like, I forgot that.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.010
- It's so Epic, like--

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:28.160
- It's so iconic though,

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:30.435
how do you forget?
- The guitar intros, the

00:09:30.435 --> 00:09:31.530
(Joey imitates intense guitar)

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.460
- I think the "One Piece" rap

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.713
is like, blocked out all
childhood English openings for me.

00:09:36.566 --> 00:09:39.310
- I had the pleasure of
not growing up with that.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:40.580
- What do you mean, pleasure?

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.253
You had the displeasure.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.350
- So my question to you then Chris,

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.670
because a lot, obviously in our circles,

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:50.760
a lot of people we know came to Japan

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:53.890
or moved to Japan, like
based on somewhat based

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.040
on their love for anime.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:56.240
And, that's us included.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.070
So like, why did you
choose to move to Japan

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:02.190
if anime wasn't like a
big reason or any reason?

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:03.240
- Yeah, that's asked a lot.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.467
I always feel like
guilty by coming to Japan

00:10:05.467 --> 00:10:07.879
and not knowing anime or Otaku culture.

00:10:07.879 --> 00:10:08.879
- I mean, it's fine.

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:12.680
- Growing up. Japan was everything to me.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.180
So, such an amazing,
mystical, wonderful country.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:16.490
Everything I used, everything I did,

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:19.060
every craze was Japanese in some way,

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.040
from Pokemon to Nintendo, Sony.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.083
My first camcorder that
I often used was Sharp.

00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:27.570
Japan always felt like a country,

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.130
I wanted to try and visit, and I go to

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.050
But being British, if you look at a map

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.000
of where Japan is and where the UK is,

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:35.945
we're not going there.

00:10:35.945 --> 00:10:37.055
(the rest laugh)

00:10:37.055 --> 00:10:39.270
It's so far. To North American viewers,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:41.390
though obviously it's
still pretty damn far.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:42.223
If you look at the map it's like,

00:10:42.223 --> 00:10:44.210
it's not that far, it's the water.

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:45.990
But Japan is so damn far.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:47.590
And so I thought, I'm not gonna go there.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:50.050
But then I thought about teaching English.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:51.630
And Japan came up as an option,

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:53.320
and it was the only country,

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.160
I ever thought I'd wanna teach English in,

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:57.370
and actually immerse myself
and learn about the culture.

00:10:57.370 --> 00:10:59.483
So in a nutshell, that was kind of it.

00:10:59.483 --> 00:11:00.843
- So even before all of that,

00:11:00.843 --> 00:11:03.440
like you were already
set on leaving the UK

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.273
at one point, or?

00:11:04.273 --> 00:11:06.270
- Absolutely, I always
wanted to live overseas

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.280
and travel somewhere in Asia.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.870
- So where would have
been your second option

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:10.779
if you never got to Japan?

00:11:10.779 --> 00:11:11.750
- I was going to ask that.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:12.583
- Second option?

00:11:12.583 --> 00:11:14.770
I don't know if there was
a second option in Asia.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:16.710
It was kind of like Japan or nothing.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:17.810
- Okay, oh, wow.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:20.030
- Just Japanese culture really excited me,

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:22.073
like all the stuff, I had seeing about it.

00:11:22.073 --> 00:11:24.080
- Because I watched your
first video last night.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.320
I was doing some research too.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.263
And the vibe you gave off almost was like,

00:11:28.263 --> 00:11:29.796
" All right lads, I'm in Japan."

00:11:29.796 --> 00:11:30.629
(all laugh)

00:11:30.629 --> 00:11:31.839
You know what I mean,
like I've just landed--

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:32.990
- Lads on tour!

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.530
- It's kinda like, I just ended in Japan.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.067
Might fuck around and stay a little bit.

00:11:37.067 --> 00:11:39.400
(all laugh)

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:41.240
So I got the impression, I just thought

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:42.510
it was like a thing where you were like

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.832
all right, fuck it, might move to Japan.

00:11:43.832 --> 00:11:45.370
You know, that's kind of like the vibe

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.370
I got from the video, I guess.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.470
But obviously hearing
that I'm kind of shocked.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.170
But then I could also see a British person

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.340
not wanting to seem like
obsessed with something

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:54.899
because that's uncool in British culture.

00:11:54.899 --> 00:11:55.732
- Yeah.

00:11:55.732 --> 00:11:57.970
- No, it's not that. I
mean the trouble is like,

00:11:57.970 --> 00:11:59.580
most people who come into
Japan, are interested

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:00.687
in the anime and stuff,

00:12:00.687 --> 00:12:02.250
and I remember doing a Japanese class

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:04.140
at university on the side, and I was like

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.349
the only one in that class
that didn't really watch anime.

00:12:06.349 --> 00:12:08.932
(Garnt laughs)

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.210
- I'm learning Japanese,

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.840
and I'm the only one
who doesn't watch anime?

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:15.481
- I was the only one in the room.

00:12:15.481 --> 00:12:18.800
And I was like, oh God,
is this gonna work?

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.467
Do you need to know
anime to live in Japan?

00:12:20.467 --> 00:12:22.978
Do you need to be into
that sort of culture?

00:12:22.978 --> 00:12:25.080
(Gigg laughs)

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.300
But now, I think I was
just drawn to like how

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.133
the culture is very different.

00:12:28.133 --> 00:12:30.900
One of the things I studied
was cultural discourse,

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.010
which is so boring, that
I never talk about it.

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.010
And I was really into cultural
discourse and language,

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:37.190
and Japan kept coming up in the studies,

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:38.327
and that was actually one reason.

00:12:38.327 --> 00:12:40.877
But I never talk that, just
because it's so boring.

00:12:43.355 --> 00:12:44.880
- I've never heard of social discourse.

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.080
So, what does that entail?

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.020
- Well, just like how,
everything in the culture

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.410
is just so different the
way people communicate,

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:54.880
so indirectly.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.643
There's such a different mindset in Japan

00:12:57.643 --> 00:13:00.520
that is so incomprehensibly different

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:02.520
to most other countries,

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:03.840
and I started learning about that,

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.850
and I really wanted to kind
of immerse myself in that.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.770
- Yeah, I mean I think
that's definitely true.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.220
Especially having moved
here and lived here

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:12.960
for a little bit, we discussed that a bit

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.060
on our last podcast
where we discussed Japan.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.590
So what was it that made
you decide to make like

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.000
YouTube videos about
Japan, or was it just,

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.043
you decided to kind of
vlog your experiences.

00:13:23.043 --> 00:13:24.880
- Did you plan that before,

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:26.065
or was that just like you turned up,

00:13:26.065 --> 00:13:26.980
and you're like this
seems like a good idea.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:28.670
- It was an idea at the
back of my head I think.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:30.540
I'd always been interested in filmmaking,

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:32.350
but kinda threw that dream away.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:33.207
Because it's quite hard to get into

00:13:33.207 --> 00:13:34.440
the film industry in the UK.

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.450
As you guys might know, and so I was like

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.070
that's not gonna happen.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:39.790
But then I go ahead and I was like,

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:40.970
I might make a YouTube channel.

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:42.490
And actually before I got to Japan,

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:45.078
there wasn't many YouTubers in Japan.

00:13:45.078 --> 00:13:46.767
And I was like, I'm really
excited, what's Japan like?

00:13:46.767 --> 00:13:49.740
I went on YouTube and there
wasn't a whole lot going on.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:52.090
No offense to the YouTubers in Japan

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:55.162
then, pre-2012, but it wasn't much.

00:13:55.162 --> 00:13:55.995
And was like okay.

00:13:55.995 --> 00:13:59.150
Maybe I can fill that void and
take my hobby of filmmaking,

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:00.490
and my dream that I've forgotten,

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.210
and actually kind of use that
and do something with it.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.817
So I hadn't watched a lot of
YouTube up until that point.

00:14:05.817 --> 00:14:08.490
And I kind of drew a lot of my influences

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.430
from British TV rather than other kind

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:12.640
of vloggers and other YouTubers.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.510
- Yeah, because like, I
think anyone can agree

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:17.660
that if you watch any one of your videos

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:19.960
and it definitely has
more of like a TV-vibe

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:21.057
than like a traditional like,

00:14:21.057 --> 00:14:22.080
"hey guys, I'm in Japan" kind of vibe.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:25.070
- It doesn't feel like
an iteration on YouTube,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:27.210
which a lot of channels that
have come into it newer,

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.007
do feel like they're
trying to emulate a style,

00:14:29.007 --> 00:14:31.400
but build on it, whereas I feel like

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.930
maybe that is what is so
unique about your take

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:35.508
on the J-vlogging scene.

00:14:35.508 --> 00:14:37.673
- I mean, I don't really have
anything against J-vlogging.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:40.687
- But like for me, the
thing that excited me

00:14:40.687 --> 00:14:43.690
was the idea of making like
a crappy low-budget TV show

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:45.820
rather than like a vlog about Japan.

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:49.580
- It seems like, it gave
off the impression of like

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:52.890
a BBC, not a BBC show,
but like a show you'd see

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:56.610
on a show or like a news
broadcast about Japan,

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:59.490
or about a different culture, and I think

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:02.830
that's how I found your content at first,

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:07.820
and you mentioned being one of
the first people to do vlogs,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.760
not vlogs, but like videos about Japan.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.477
- High quality vlogs.

00:15:11.477 --> 00:15:12.960
- Do you remember the scene back then

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:14.310
of what vlogs were like?

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:16.040
It was like the shaky cam.
- Back when, 2012?

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.760
- Yeah, 2012.
- Right, oh Jesus.

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.157
- I mean, yeah.

00:15:19.157 --> 00:15:21.578
- It's like FPS Russia and
that's like all I remember.

00:15:21.578 --> 00:15:23.050
(the rest laugh)

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:23.883
Just blowing shit up.

00:15:23.883 --> 00:15:26.910
- I mean FPS Russia was
probably like the high.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.973
- Epic Meal Time.

00:15:29.258 --> 00:15:30.220
- Those are good times.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.770
I feel like that was the golden
era of YouTube, in a sense.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.600
- I don't know, I feel like every era,

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.210
has it's own thing to it
and I think it's easy,

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.540
to look back and be like,
that was the best time.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.930
Forget about all those
people filming their TV,

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:43.838
playing cards, like looking at it.

00:15:43.838 --> 00:15:45.550
(all laugh)

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:46.880
How could we call that the golden age?

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:47.713
You know what I mean.

00:15:47.713 --> 00:15:49.587
- Stick a camcorder in a
shoe to like prop it up,

00:15:49.587 --> 00:15:51.150
make it level.

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:53.680
- Because I think, was Sharla In Japan

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.183
the only vlogger really before.

00:15:56.183 --> 00:15:58.253
- There was a few. There was Micaela,

00:15:59.599 --> 00:16:01.005
Rachel and Jun, actually

00:16:01.005 --> 00:16:04.160
there was Gimmeabreakman, Victor,

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:06.143
and there was Tokyocooney.

00:16:08.110 --> 00:16:09.600
- I've come to know,

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:12.380
a lot of the J-vloggers
that live here, like since.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.540
The J-vlogging community
is so small, in a sense,

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.160
that once you know a few,

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:18.850
you basically know everybody,
at that point, eventually.

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:22.150
And like, meeting Chris
and like meeting a bunch

00:16:22.150 --> 00:16:25.447
of other newer and older vloggers,

00:16:25.447 --> 00:16:27.490
it's really interesting to see

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:30.180
just how big it actually is.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.280
Because on the surface,
I think, if you don't

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:34.990
know a lot of them, it
does just seem like Chris

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:36.970
and a few other people.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:37.899
Whereas like there are just

00:16:37.899 --> 00:16:39.667
so many J-vloggers out there.
- And that's how

00:16:39.667 --> 00:16:40.500
YouTube works anywhere.

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.030
You can kind of easily stay
in a bubble, if you want to.

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:44.610
- Yeah.
- Yeah, definitely.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.890
- How do you feel about the
current J-vlogging scene though?

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:49.230
- I don't know really.

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:50.250
I'm friends with a lot of them,

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.233
but I don't watch that much.

00:16:52.233 --> 00:16:53.961
Because I live in Japan,

00:16:53.961 --> 00:16:56.443
I don't really want
watch other Japan stuff.

00:16:56.443 --> 00:17:00.230
I do my stuff, I spend ages
working on a video in a room.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:01.460
I upload it and then I go off

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:03.961
and drive around the mountains and go.

00:17:03.961 --> 00:17:04.794
(laughs)

00:17:04.794 --> 00:17:05.627
Don't quote me on that!

00:17:05.627 --> 00:17:07.710
(laughs)

00:17:08.805 --> 00:17:11.210
I've kind of felt like to be disconnected

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:12.320
from it a little bit.

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:15.240
- Do you ever like see a video
made by another J-vlogger,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.330
you're like fuck! I wanted to do that.

00:17:17.330 --> 00:17:18.487
- Yeah, sometimes.

00:17:18.487 --> 00:17:20.403
- Do you just scrap it if
that happens, or what's the--

00:17:20.403 --> 00:17:22.080
- I remember I was thinking of doing

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:23.630
a day in the life series, and then

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:25.010
a YouTuber called Paolo--

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:26.638
- Yeah, I've seen his.

00:17:26.638 --> 00:17:28.350
- And I was like, he's
done it. All right, fine.

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:29.680
- You can't do a carbon copy, right?

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.650
- Yeah, I can't. And that's fair enough.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:33.522
He's done it well. If he didn't
do it well, though I think--

00:17:33.522 --> 00:17:34.355
- But you can do it better,

00:17:34.355 --> 00:17:37.365
- But you did something
similar, like with yourself.

00:17:37.365 --> 00:17:40.143
Like you did like a day in the life,

00:17:40.143 --> 00:17:42.850
of like abroad in Japan, on a bicycle.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:44.230
No.
- No.

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:46.474
- I think the thumbnail was
like you in a Shinkansen.

00:17:46.474 --> 00:17:48.150
- Yeah, I did it once, many years ago.

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:50.674
Maybe he got the inspiration
for his series from that.

00:17:50.674 --> 00:17:51.507
(laughs)

00:17:51.507 --> 00:17:54.150
We never know. He definitely did.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:57.250
- I feel like whereas,
maybe like a channel

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.720
like Paolo is, I feel like it's more,

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:02.680
really 100% based on what
is happening in the video

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:03.700
and what's being shown.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:04.630
But I feel like at
least with your channel,

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:06.770
there is a lot of your
humor, which is kind of

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.835
crucial to the style, and to the video.

00:18:09.835 --> 00:18:11.350
So I feel like maybe if you did do it,

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:12.490
if you added your own humor into it.

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.660
But you don't want to get
in the way of the thing?

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:15.500
- I know if I did it,

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:16.550
then loads of people would comment--

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:17.925
- Oh, this is rubbish.
- Yeah,

00:18:17.925 --> 00:18:19.820
- This is rubbish.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.400
So that's my main thinking.

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:23.233
But he's done it well,

00:18:23.233 --> 00:18:24.450
he's covered it like interesting people.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.113
When I say day in my
life, I don't mean me.

00:18:26.113 --> 00:18:27.357
A day in my life is rubbish.

00:18:27.357 --> 00:18:30.432
It's like me sitting in a
room matching my socks, going.

00:18:30.432 --> 00:18:31.716
(laughing)

00:18:31.716 --> 00:18:34.080
That's literally my life, that's reality.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:34.913
- Matching up socks?

00:18:34.913 --> 00:18:37.083
- Matching up socks, it's doing nothing.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:39.980
- I feel like exactly the same,

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.380
where there was a point
when I tried to watch

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:45.360
like every other anime YouTuber,

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:46.870
or everyone in my sphere

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:47.830
and then it just got to a point

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:49.253
when it just became stressful.

00:18:49.253 --> 00:18:50.850
Because you just watch other YouTubers

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:53.723
and you're like, man, I
wanted to do that topic.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.420
And now, like pretty much, I just keep up

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:00.200
with what my mates are
doing or some other people.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.690
But did you guys ever go
through that phase as well?

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.800
- I did but then like
very quickly I realised

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.130
just how, again, same with J-Vlogging,

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:11.130
just how broad anime YouTubing is,

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.630
and how many topics that were covered.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:15.200
At that time I was like, man,

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.033
I wanted to do that idea,

00:19:16.033 --> 00:19:16.866
or I wanted to do that idea.

00:19:16.866 --> 00:19:19.037
But then after a while,
I was just like, fuck it.

00:19:19.037 --> 00:19:21.093
People have already done
this, I'll fucking do it.

00:19:21.093 --> 00:19:22.487
And like, I'll put my own twist on it

00:19:22.487 --> 00:19:24.180
and I'm sure my audience will enjoy it.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:26.850
And if they're so willing to be like,

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:28.340
this is just a copy of so-and-so,

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:29.173
and I'm just like.

00:19:29.173 --> 00:19:30.750
- I had a problem, I was doing a video

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:31.730
earlier this year about,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.360
things I'll never understand about Japan.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:35.180
And when I was working, I
was like, wait a minute,

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:37.356
someone has done this
already, and it's Joey.

00:19:37.356 --> 00:19:38.877
(others laugh)

00:19:38.877 --> 00:19:39.710
Because yours was like,

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:40.750
"12 things I'll never
understand about Japan",

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:42.378
mine's got 10, and I was like shit,

00:19:42.378 --> 00:19:44.430
I can't do that.

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:45.960
- 13 things about Japan
I'll never understand.

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:47.350
- Just add an extra one.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.363
- So I think, I'll just call
it Why I will never understand

00:19:49.363 --> 00:19:51.533
and that-
- And it still did well.

00:19:52.914 --> 00:19:54.670
- I don't think I ever really
watched many YouTubers,

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:56.650
aside from you two, I think.

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:58.003
And I never really worried
about overlapping at all.

00:19:58.003 --> 00:19:59.520
Because I was pretty confident

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:02.890
that I was doing the stupidest
shit that was available.

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:04.670
So I was like, no one else is willing

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:06.030
to embarrass themselves as me,

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:06.863
so I'm pretty sure I've got this.

00:20:06.863 --> 00:20:08.560
- You are kind of an anomaly in that.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:10.920
- You were called The 93% for a reason.

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.080
- Do you know why they call him The 93%?

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:14.229
- Because he is a white male.

00:20:14.229 --> 00:20:16.562
(all laugh)

00:20:18.909 --> 00:20:20.540
- I don't know.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:22.310
- At one point, my audience was,

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:23.800
well I'm not gonna say for some reason,

00:20:23.800 --> 00:20:27.345
I know why, was 93% female, at some point.

00:20:27.345 --> 00:20:30.020
- Which in the Anime YouTube
world was unheard of.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:30.883
- Because it was normally,
the other way around.

00:20:30.883 --> 00:20:33.800
- It's a massive sausage
fest, essentially.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:34.633
- Normally it is.

00:20:34.633 --> 00:20:35.970
It's dudes arguing about you know,

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:37.920
which Naruto power up is the strongest.

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:40.120
- Wow, that's quite impressive.

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:40.953
Is it like that now?

00:20:40.953 --> 00:20:43.853
- No, it's like 60-40 now?

00:20:45.502 --> 00:20:47.160
- Still quite impressive.

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:47.993
- At the time, I guess.

00:20:47.993 --> 00:20:51.425
Because I was covering mainly
anime shows while doing stuff.

00:20:51.425 --> 00:20:53.250
I was doing my prank
calls as anime characters

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:56.240
from shows that were like
more female-oriented.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:20:57.670
- And that's the secret?

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.510
- Doing the British,
like the British butler

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:01.957
like Sebastian. yes, you know.

00:21:01.957 --> 00:21:03.300
I would like call up
McDonald's and tell them,

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:05.343
oh my gosh, it's not working.

00:21:05.343 --> 00:21:06.570
(others laugh)

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.927
- And I think like the reason why,

00:21:08.927 --> 00:21:10.250
Connor did excel in that

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:12.120
is because again, he was tapping into

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.090
an audience, like a female demographic

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:16.800
that literally none of
us were tapping into.

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.006
- I feel like a lot of people on YouTube

00:21:18.006 --> 00:21:21.221
are really unoriginal (laughs).

00:21:21.221 --> 00:21:24.080
Like they never think outside of like,

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.740
if it's the anime community at that time,

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.030
it was either talk about anime,

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.030
or make a show where you talk over anime

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:31.289
and make them say things
they wouldn't say.

00:21:31.289 --> 00:21:33.170
And no one was really trying to think,

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:34.760
what else could you do with this medium

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.254
other than talk about
shows and do nothing.

00:21:37.254 --> 00:21:38.580
And I was like, I kind of like

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.930
the abridging, which is
like, you re-dub anime

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:43.430
but make them say horrible things.

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.800
But I didn't like how that
took like a month to make.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.100
So I was like, all right,
I'll go in the middle.

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:48.210
I'll take these characters

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:51.230
and just prank call people
with them, and do the voices.

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:52.470
And kind of, was like a happy medium

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:54.900
of like messing around,
but also I can interject

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.420
a lot of my personality into it,

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:57.370
slowly built it up.

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:00.470
But I haven't done prank
calls in so long though.

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:01.303
- I was about to say.

00:22:01.303 --> 00:22:03.710
- Because for me, like I.

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:05.130
Do you guys remember how you started

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:08.350
to find your style, because
when I started YouTube,

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:10.660
mine was completely,
here's I going to try,

00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.060
and rip off zero punctuation,

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:13.946
and this is just my style.

00:22:13.946 --> 00:22:15.980
- Do you remember your old
Twitter bio, because I do.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:17.980
- I do not.
- His old Twitter bio was--

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:20.550
because I think I remember
clicking on it for some reason.

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:24.780
It was like, I'm that other
fast-talking British guy that--

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:25.860
- It was actually, no way!

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.690
- I think it was like I'm the other--

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:30.373
- Oh, that's so embarrassing!

00:22:32.143 --> 00:22:33.463
(Gigg laughing)
- Not Yahtzee.

00:22:33.463 --> 00:22:34.530
- Because that was always my top comment,

00:22:34.530 --> 00:22:37.350
so I was just like, let's
refer to this in the bio

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:39.890
but the more I did videos I guess,

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:41.660
the more that my own style evolved.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:44.500
Because I started YouTube 2007,

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.610
so mine was like near

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:48.300
the beginning of YouTube.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:50.280
So there wasn't really anyone else

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.580
to play off of, so I just kind of copied

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:53.890
who I was watching at the time.

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:56.593
And there wasn't many
creators on the platform.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.730
But in terms of you guys,
how did you find your style,

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:03.050
how did you start out, I guess?

00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:04.487
- I mean, I don't know really.

00:23:04.487 --> 00:23:07.040
- Do you almost feel like YouTube style

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:08.910
is like you just try a bunch of things

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:10.090
and then one or two of them work,

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:12.640
and you just iterate it
and then you try again

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:14.440
with a bunch of iterations
of like that thing

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:15.490
that you did yourself.

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.430
Not copying other people but like,

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:18.555
so you have a success as one thing

00:23:18.555 --> 00:23:20.310
and then you're like, okay,
what worked about that?

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:22.790
How do I then try a bunch of new things.

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:24.230
And then slowly you start to figure out,

00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:25.920
this isn't working, this isn't working.

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:26.810
I don't know, what do you think?

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:28.270
Because yours is just very different,

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:30.240
because obviously, lots of more comedy.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:31.790
- Mine's more based off British TV,

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:33.673
like Charlie Brooker, for example.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:37.463
- I love Charlie Brooker. Oh my God.

00:23:37.463 --> 00:23:40.660
I take inspiration from
screen writers as well.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:43.510
My year end reviews,
was directly influenced

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:45.430
by Charlie Brooker, fucking fantastic.

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:47.040
- And that is one of my key influences,

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:48.740
so that's something I would have watched.

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:49.850
And then when I sat
down in front the camera

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:51.630
I thought, that's what I want to channel.

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:54.340
And when I'm on camera,
it's kind of not me.

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:56.340
It's more like a, kind of more

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:59.130
obviously energetic
sarcastic bits, individual.

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:02.310
I'm not that monstrous off, I might be.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.780
- I feel like your style

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:04.970
sometimes it's almost like reminiscent

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:06.310
of like David Mitchell.

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:09.060
In terms of that very like dry sarcasm,

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.470
it's just so beautiful.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:11.450
I love David Mitchell.

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:13.016
You don't think so?

00:24:13.016 --> 00:24:14.670
- I'd like that.

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:16.580
- Without the depressing look sometimes

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:17.413
that Dave Mitchell has his eyes.

00:24:17.413 --> 00:24:20.541
- I don't know, man, I've been
on a four-day trip with him.

00:24:20.541 --> 00:24:22.040
(all laugh)

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.453
I saw some pretty depressing, sad faces.

00:24:27.502 --> 00:24:29.710
- I think just British
TV is my main influence.

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:31.810
I wouldn't say, actually,
I've been influenced

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:33.740
by another YouTube channel
as such as a result.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:35.520
But what I have found is over the years,

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.240
I started off quite
strong in that character

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:38.870
and I lost it, because I started going

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:41.170
from commentating on things in a room

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:42.557
to doing more travel stuff.

00:24:42.557 --> 00:24:45.248
And you can't be a
cynical, sarcastic dickhead

00:24:45.248 --> 00:24:46.963
when you're meeting people, like,

00:24:46.963 --> 00:24:49.767
this is your dinner and
I'm like fuck off love.

00:24:49.767 --> 00:24:50.600
(others laugh)

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.693
Thank you very much (speaks
in foreign language.)

00:24:52.693 --> 00:24:54.240
Like you have to be nicer,

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.450
when you are up and about doing things.

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:56.630
So I lost that.

00:24:56.630 --> 00:24:58.390
But this year, by virtue of the virus

00:24:58.390 --> 00:24:59.910
by virtue of being indoors more,

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:02.840
I've kind of got back to
rediscovering that character

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:05.401
and who I actually enjoy being on camera.

00:25:05.401 --> 00:25:07.062
- I think it's weird you say that

00:25:07.062 --> 00:25:10.750
because I did a video in like,
well, two videos I've done.

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:12.130
Like I'm trying to do some videos

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:15.070
outside in Japan, like out
of my bedroom basically.

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:16.980
- I did a video in a host club, and--

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:18.470
- It's a good video. I really liked it.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:20.540
- Thank you. And that
was really difficult,

00:25:20.540 --> 00:25:22.617
because, my back of my head was like,

00:25:22.617 --> 00:25:25.060
I want to crack jokes, at the
dumb stuff that they doing,

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:26.200
or the really cringy moments.

00:25:26.200 --> 00:25:28.450
But at the same time, I
don't wanna offend them.

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:30.600
So it's kind of striking
that balance of like,

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.920
I'm playing the fool, where I'm like

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:33.103
that's odd, isn't it?

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.210
I'm like that's kind of strange,

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:37.790
but then just moving on.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.220
- It's a tough one, you can't,

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:40.860
that sort of cynical sarcastic side.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.140
- You kind of strike that balance.

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:44.010
- You've kind of gotta be like,

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:45.270
rhetorical in a sense,

00:25:45.270 --> 00:25:47.870
and not just be like
directly, like that's dumb.

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:48.830
- Because if you're in your bedroom,

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:50.530
and your are watching a
video or you are commentating

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:52.130
on something, you can really just go in

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:53.770
and like full on jokes, right.

00:25:53.770 --> 00:25:55.340
So it's trying to channel that almost

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:56.650
that British sense of like,

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.820
just doing a little, you know little jab,

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.960
but saying a point
that's not a jab, right?

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:01.903
That kind of thing, where it's like,

00:26:01.903 --> 00:26:03.520
this is strange, isn't it?

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:04.620
- Let's talk about that,

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:07.660
what was the whole
experience like for you?

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:09.810
Is there anything off camera or anything--

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:11.720
- I only heard the stories
you told me that was.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:13.530
- There was a lot of things.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:15.390
Because we all went there to scout

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:16.223
it the day before, didn't we?

00:26:16.223 --> 00:26:17.056
- Yeah.

00:26:17.056 --> 00:26:18.090
- Just we happened to be in Shinjuku,

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:19.720
and I was gonna film there, the next day.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:21.060
And we were like , can we just walk

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:22.420
up to this host club and see what's up

00:26:22.420 --> 00:26:24.880
and it like, looked sketch, right?

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:26.623
- It was pretty fucking dirty.

00:26:26.623 --> 00:26:29.410
- They were very nice to
me, but I will be honest

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.627
