01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:37.690
- I've only gotten into
doing that recently.

01:01:37.690 --> 01:01:39.220
Before, it was just making the video

01:01:39.220 --> 01:01:40.930
that had a good concept and figure it out.

01:01:40.930 --> 01:01:42.900
But I realized, I was
getting in situations,

01:01:42.900 --> 01:01:44.870
where videos weren't doing to hot,

01:01:44.870 --> 01:01:46.897
because people weren't clicking on it.

01:01:46.897 --> 01:01:49.260
And then once I got into the
habit of like, get the title

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:51.490
then make the video around
it. almost all times, right.

01:01:51.490 --> 01:01:54.440
It's horrible to say that, but
you kind of have to do that.

01:01:54.440 --> 01:01:56.470
- Title and thumbnail is by far,

01:01:56.470 --> 01:01:59.080
the most important thing

01:01:59.080 --> 01:02:00.550
about being a successful YouTuber.

01:02:00.550 --> 01:02:02.350
- It is sad that it's
gotten to that point,

01:02:02.350 --> 01:02:04.310
where you can't just have like a banger,

01:02:04.310 --> 01:02:06.550
like if you get a low click through rate,

01:02:06.550 --> 01:02:08.870
YouTube's like, no, that's
not getting recommended.

01:02:08.870 --> 01:02:12.320
You've got 3% less click
through rate than normal.

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:16.199
- Even some courageous who don't try,

01:02:16.199 --> 01:02:18.100
with their title and thumbnail as much

01:02:18.100 --> 01:02:20.770
as the other YouTubers, that's
a brand in it of itself.

01:02:20.770 --> 01:02:21.820
You know what I mean?

01:02:22.757 --> 01:02:25.120
- There some YouTubers
that can literally title

01:02:25.120 --> 01:02:27.030
and thumbnail a video, anything.

01:02:27.030 --> 01:02:29.480
And they just built that trust,

01:02:29.480 --> 01:02:30.720
they are like they'll click through it.

01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:31.990
And I like to think that

01:02:31.990 --> 01:02:34.670
your channel is very much
like that in a sense,

01:02:34.670 --> 01:02:37.320
because I don't really look
at an Abroad in Japan video,

01:02:37.320 --> 01:02:39.480
and think that's click bait.

01:02:39.480 --> 01:02:42.660
Like I know from the get-go,

01:02:42.660 --> 01:02:43.750
what I'm going to get into,

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:45.841
when I see the title and thumbnail.

01:02:45.841 --> 01:02:47.850
- But like how long have
you been doing YouTube?

01:02:47.850 --> 01:02:49.210
- I've been doing it, eight years

01:02:49.210 --> 01:02:51.710
as of the August, this year.

01:02:51.710 --> 01:02:53.150
- 2012.

01:02:53.150 --> 01:02:56.263
- Yeah. But I've only made
200 videos, I think so far.

01:02:57.330 --> 01:03:00.023
I make far less videos than you guys.

01:03:00.023 --> 01:03:02.020
So I have to make every video really count

01:03:02.020 --> 01:03:03.610
for something, has to be that video.

01:03:03.610 --> 01:03:06.890
- I think a lot of people,
wouldn't say that they are,

01:03:06.890 --> 01:03:09.260
I know a lot of people here
who really speak really highly

01:03:09.260 --> 01:03:11.023
of your content, so it makes sense.

01:03:13.220 --> 01:03:14.630
But going on off that,

01:03:14.630 --> 01:03:19.370
do you feel like the quality
ball raises each year

01:03:19.370 --> 01:03:21.320
with like content that's
being made in Japan?

01:03:21.320 --> 01:03:23.140
Like the kind of vlog stuff?

01:03:23.140 --> 01:03:26.050
Or do you feel like you're
already on top of that?

01:03:26.050 --> 01:03:28.170
- I remember, about four years in

01:03:28.170 --> 01:03:29.267
there was a channel that popped up called

01:03:29.267 --> 01:03:32.080
"Only in Japan" run by
a John Daub, a cool guy.

01:03:32.080 --> 01:03:33.303
And he raised the bar.

01:03:33.303 --> 01:03:36.380
Like I used to just be a
vlog, kind of vlogging star.

01:03:36.380 --> 01:03:37.875
Had TV production quality,

01:03:37.875 --> 01:03:39.613
and I was like, shit, that's the future.

01:03:39.613 --> 01:03:40.830
So I raise my bar to try

01:03:40.830 --> 01:03:42.920
and reach that kind of level of quality.

01:03:42.920 --> 01:03:44.190
- And I I've noticed since then

01:03:44.190 --> 01:03:45.960
a lot of channels also started to slowly

01:03:45.960 --> 01:03:47.120
like creeping up as well,

01:03:47.120 --> 01:03:48.650
with the similar kind of production value.

01:03:48.650 --> 01:03:50.680
- Well, we'll say it's production value,

01:03:50.680 --> 01:03:52.350
isn't as important as people think it is.

01:03:52.350 --> 01:03:53.930
The main thing is just having content

01:03:53.930 --> 01:03:55.110
that people want to watch.

01:03:55.110 --> 01:03:57.130
I've made lots of videos
out and about traveling

01:03:57.130 --> 01:03:58.980
to interesting places, but the videos

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:00.320
that always did the best

01:04:00.320 --> 01:04:01.480
it's just me sitting in a room

01:04:01.480 --> 01:04:03.470
giving a commentary on something.

01:04:03.470 --> 01:04:04.990
- I mean, that's the thing
at the end of the day, right?

01:04:04.990 --> 01:04:07.140
It doesn't matter how
good of a camera you have,

01:04:07.140 --> 01:04:09.360
how good of a mic, how
good your editing is.

01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:11.130
If you have a personality of a brick wall,

01:04:11.130 --> 01:04:12.160
no one's gonna fucking watch you.

01:04:12.160 --> 01:04:14.820
- I mean, that's YouTube really, right?

01:04:14.820 --> 01:04:17.160
If you want to get a
sterilized document of a place

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:19.080
or documentary we can
watch something else.

01:04:19.080 --> 01:04:20.380
I mean, that's even like, Louis Theroux,

01:04:20.380 --> 01:04:22.979
you watch him as well.
- He's lovely.

01:04:22.979 --> 01:04:25.200
- I fucking love Louis Theroux,
I would marry Louis Theroux.

01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:26.130
- He is very outstanding.

01:04:26.130 --> 01:04:28.280
- He is though right.
Because of the way that

01:04:28.280 --> 01:04:30.520
he doesn't like obstruct.
- Judge.

01:04:30.520 --> 01:04:31.353
- He doesn't judge, right.

01:04:31.353 --> 01:04:32.850
He doesn't get in the
way of the documentary

01:04:32.850 --> 01:04:34.570
but he gets just enough personality,

01:04:34.570 --> 01:04:36.820
where like you want it
to be his documentary.

01:04:37.944 --> 01:04:39.490
Sometimes you watch a
documentary, you are like

01:04:39.490 --> 01:04:41.190
I wish Louis Theroux made this.

01:04:41.190 --> 01:04:42.703
It could have been good.

01:04:43.850 --> 01:04:44.890
I feel like you've like

01:04:44.890 --> 01:04:47.340
almost got a really
good balance with that.

01:04:47.340 --> 01:04:48.980
I feel like you interject your personality

01:04:48.980 --> 01:04:51.960
just enough where it's like,
it's really cements that like,

01:04:51.960 --> 01:04:54.636
I really wish Abroad
in Japan covered this.

01:04:54.636 --> 01:04:56.270
- I just feel, with a good video,

01:04:56.270 --> 01:04:57.860
it should be entertaining and educational,

01:04:57.860 --> 01:04:59.510
there's a fine line to balance.

01:04:59.510 --> 01:05:00.630
And that's the most important thing

01:05:00.630 --> 01:05:01.710
I always try and focus on.

01:05:01.710 --> 01:05:03.260
- And that's what I
found really interesting

01:05:03.260 --> 01:05:05.020
when I joined you for
Journey across Japan.

01:05:05.020 --> 01:05:08.540
Because you brought up the whole thing--

01:05:08.540 --> 01:05:11.385
- Such quality content,
strapping a pillow to a bicycle.

01:05:11.385 --> 01:05:14.750
- When you told me before
the whole thing started,

01:05:14.750 --> 01:05:17.190
that like," I'm going to try
and release a video every day".

01:05:17.190 --> 01:05:19.480
And I thought back to
your production quality,

01:05:19.480 --> 01:05:20.965
and I'm like, good fucking laughter.

01:05:20.965 --> 01:05:21.902
(laughs)

01:05:21.902 --> 01:05:23.320
- When I found out that you were editing

01:05:23.320 --> 01:05:26.500
your own videos for that, I
was like, is this guy insane?

01:05:26.500 --> 01:05:28.720
- I knew it. One time,
Joey left me, one week,

01:05:28.720 --> 01:05:29.553
I did the cycling--

01:05:29.553 --> 01:05:30.893
- He was editing those videos and cycling.

01:05:30.893 --> 01:05:32.680
- What!
- I did the cycling

01:05:32.680 --> 01:05:34.090
across Japan for like two months.

01:05:34.090 --> 01:05:36.917
And I was cycling,
filming and editing it all

01:05:36.917 --> 01:05:38.179
at the same time.

01:05:38.179 --> 01:05:40.570
Because I'm a real control freaking idiot.

01:05:40.570 --> 01:05:42.830
Even though I had like two
people who are willing to edit

01:05:42.830 --> 01:05:46.239
with me on the journey
in the car, I was like,

01:05:46.239 --> 01:05:47.645
I'll do it. Don't worry.

01:05:47.645 --> 01:05:50.880
- I remember after the
first day I sat with you

01:05:50.880 --> 01:05:53.240
in your hotel room as you were like,

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:54.980
starting to edit over there.

01:05:54.980 --> 01:05:58.133
And I'm just like, you gonna
be able to get less sleep?

01:05:59.830 --> 01:06:01.430
I think you stayed up till like,

01:06:02.450 --> 01:06:05.290
We had to get up , I think
the next day at like--

01:06:05.290 --> 01:06:07.000
- Nine o'clock, 10 o'clock.
- 8 or 9 a.m.

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:08.400
It was really early,

01:06:08.400 --> 01:06:11.680
and fucking Chris had
stayed up till like 5:00 AM.

01:06:11.680 --> 01:06:13.210
- What the fuck?

01:06:13.210 --> 01:06:16.087
- He had gotten maybe like
four hours sleep at most.

01:06:16.087 --> 01:06:17.470
And he's like, all right, guys,

01:06:17.470 --> 01:06:19.871
about to do a 15 kilometer cycle.

01:06:19.871 --> 01:06:22.243
- How many times did
you do that on the trip?

01:06:22.243 --> 01:06:23.890
- A lot. Most of the first.

01:06:23.890 --> 01:06:25.616
- How the fuck did you write that?

01:06:25.616 --> 01:06:26.966
(Garnt laughs)

01:06:26.966 --> 01:06:29.890
- What happen is, originally
the videos came out every day,

01:06:29.890 --> 01:06:31.682
then it's like every two
days, then every four,

01:06:31.682 --> 01:06:33.609
about the end, then about once a month.

01:06:33.609 --> 01:06:34.849
It was kinda like getting
longer and longer.

01:06:34.849 --> 01:06:35.790
It's very stupid.

01:06:35.790 --> 01:06:36.890
- I think you would burn yourself.

01:06:36.890 --> 01:06:40.762
That sounds like an absolute,
like fast track department.

01:06:40.762 --> 01:06:43.030
- By the time Jay was
there and Joey had left,

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:44.193
I was fucked.

01:06:45.073 --> 01:06:49.294
- I saw the rapid decline.
- The life.

01:06:49.294 --> 01:06:50.127
- It was like mental health.
- Leave my eyes.

01:06:50.127 --> 01:06:51.200
- You know what broke my heart,

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:53.953
is when, is it with
Emma that you recorded.

01:06:56.720 --> 01:06:58.620
Did you record a video with her that you--

01:06:58.620 --> 01:07:00.260
- Yeah. She came along for three days

01:07:00.260 --> 01:07:01.627
and we filmed the video.

01:07:01.627 --> 01:07:02.800
But I was like, oh I.

01:07:02.800 --> 01:07:05.446
But by that point I've
been cycling two months,

01:07:05.446 --> 01:07:06.279
1000 kilometers.

01:07:06.279 --> 01:07:07.112
- Do you wanna explain what "Journey

01:07:07.112 --> 01:07:08.207
in Japan" is in case someone for reason.

01:07:08.207 --> 01:07:10.010
- "Journey across Japan",
cycling across Japan,

01:07:10.010 --> 01:07:12.680
from Yamagata to Kagoshima
about 2000 kilometers.

01:07:12.680 --> 01:07:14.870
And the idea was to film every single day,

01:07:14.870 --> 01:07:16.973
and make a video, and
show people bits of Japan

01:07:16.973 --> 01:07:18.610
that you don't normally see.

01:07:18.610 --> 01:07:21.120
By the time Emma joined,
he was the last guest,

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:23.107
in Kishi. I was just dead.

01:07:23.107 --> 01:07:25.470
(mumbles)

01:07:25.470 --> 01:07:28.900
I was like, can sleep now and is awful.

01:07:28.900 --> 01:07:29.733
- It's such a shame.
- It was a real shame.

01:07:29.733 --> 01:07:32.420
- It was such a personality.
- It was a real shame.

01:07:32.420 --> 01:07:35.170
I feel guilty about
that. I had to apologize

01:07:35.170 --> 01:07:36.820
to Emma yesterday when I met her.

01:07:40.540 --> 01:07:42.820
- My heart broke, because
I can only imagine like

01:07:42.820 --> 01:07:44.640
you'd must've been at your
end of your end of the road.

01:07:44.640 --> 01:07:45.980
- I was at the end of everything.

01:07:45.980 --> 01:07:47.760
- And like just having like that

01:07:47.760 --> 01:07:49.416
just unusable footage would
just be like, I'm done.

01:07:49.416 --> 01:07:51.390
- It was a real shame.

01:07:51.390 --> 01:07:54.190
But at the end of the day,
it was mostly a success.

01:07:54.190 --> 01:07:55.810
We had 28 episodes by the end,

01:07:55.810 --> 01:07:58.003
and the videos with
Joey, it was interesting

01:07:58.003 --> 01:07:59.850
because in the second half the journey,

01:07:59.850 --> 01:08:03.180
we went through like
Kyoto, Osaka, Hiroshima.

01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.964
And then Joey, when he joined
us, he was like nothing.

01:08:05.964 --> 01:08:09.710
- It was literally four
episodes of Jack Shit.

01:08:09.710 --> 01:08:10.950
- The first video we filmed,

01:08:10.950 --> 01:08:13.200
the biggest nuclear
power plant in the world.

01:08:15.494 --> 01:08:17.190
And then there was nothing,
so we had to improvise

01:08:17.190 --> 01:08:19.673
and make like commercials
about energy jelly.

01:08:21.086 --> 01:08:22.910
- I was watching that, and I was like,

01:08:22.910 --> 01:08:24.460
what's going on here?

01:08:24.460 --> 01:08:25.680
- I was really excited with the idea

01:08:25.680 --> 01:08:28.650
of taking a place where
there's nothing really going on

01:08:28.650 --> 01:08:31.428
and trying to infuse some
sort of creative component.

01:08:31.428 --> 01:08:35.260
- It was like a creative
challenge because like..

01:08:35.260 --> 01:08:37.640
- We had challenges every day,
on top of everything else.

01:08:37.640 --> 01:08:39.140
So I had challenges
every day and the first,

01:08:39.140 --> 01:08:40.800
and Joey joined and the challenge was,

01:08:40.800 --> 01:08:43.623
make a commercial in a day, 24 hours.

01:08:44.740 --> 01:08:46.149
How the fuck are we going to do that?

01:08:46.149 --> 01:08:46.982
There's nothing here.

01:08:46.982 --> 01:08:48.530
So we quickly have to be really creative.

01:08:48.530 --> 01:08:51.040
We find a train station where
there's a real train carriage,

01:08:51.040 --> 01:08:51.983
you can go in.

01:08:51.983 --> 01:08:52.960
- And that was purely by chance.

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:54.190
- That was so lucky.

01:08:54.190 --> 01:08:56.680
- That was so lucky.
Because we had no idea

01:08:56.680 --> 01:08:58.300
that this station had just

01:08:58.300 --> 01:09:00.050
a fucking empty train carriage

01:09:00.050 --> 01:09:02.560
like as a thing and
was just like, perfect.

01:09:02.560 --> 01:09:04.220
- And then the challenge,

01:09:04.220 --> 01:09:06.120
we had to be about energy jelly, right?

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:06.953
So we already had that,

01:09:06.953 --> 01:09:08.480
it was the only thing we had to go on.

01:09:08.480 --> 01:09:10.810
And then we just somehow
pulled it off in a day.

01:09:10.810 --> 01:09:11.643
- So it really felt well.

01:09:11.643 --> 01:09:13.890
- It was like my favorite
video from the trip.

01:09:13.890 --> 01:09:16.360
- So how did the idea of
the travel across Japan,

01:09:16.360 --> 01:09:17.193
like come out?

01:09:17.193 --> 01:09:18.470
- I was gonna ask the exact same question.

01:09:18.470 --> 01:09:20.585
I was like, me and Connor
are on the same wavelength.

01:09:20.585 --> 01:09:22.270
- At what point was that,

01:09:22.270 --> 01:09:24.460
you thought about it, at
what point were you like,

01:09:24.460 --> 01:09:26.310
I'm going to actually stop
pursuing saying this up,

01:09:26.310 --> 01:09:27.323
and after that how did
it go all the way around.

01:09:27.323 --> 01:09:30.170
- I've being doing a documentary,
about my friend Natsuki

01:09:30.170 --> 01:09:32.830
and that took like
months to film and edit.

01:09:32.830 --> 01:09:34.390
- Still my favorite video.

01:09:34.390 --> 01:09:38.560
- And I was editing, for like
two or three months in a room.

01:09:38.560 --> 01:09:40.160
Just like, why am I doing this?

01:09:40.160 --> 01:09:41.780
And by the end, I was
like, I need to go out.

01:09:41.780 --> 01:09:42.950
I need to do something.

01:09:42.950 --> 01:09:45.428
I'd always want to travel
across Japan, by bicycle.

01:09:45.428 --> 01:09:46.920
And I thought, this could be fun.

01:09:46.920 --> 01:09:48.847
And that's pretty much how it came about.

01:09:48.847 --> 01:09:50.490
And that was it, pretty much.

01:09:50.490 --> 01:09:53.030
- This sounds like Casey Neistat,

01:09:53.030 --> 01:09:54.690
couldn't keep the schedule.
You know what I mean?

01:09:54.690 --> 01:09:56.023
- That's kind of wild.

01:09:57.528 --> 01:09:58.910
- Hearing that you still
edit your own videos,

01:09:58.910 --> 01:10:00.207
do you still edit your videos?

01:10:00.207 --> 01:10:02.090
- I do and I enjoy that.

01:10:02.090 --> 01:10:04.720
Like for me, the least favorite
part of making a video,

01:10:04.720 --> 01:10:06.320
is the presenting aspect.

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:07.450
- That's my favorite part, I think.

01:10:07.450 --> 01:10:09.530
- You guys like clearly like
really great presenters,

01:10:09.530 --> 01:10:11.657
You enjoy that. For me, I do enjoy it,

01:10:11.657 --> 01:10:14.503
but the bit that I enjoy
is the filmmaking aspect.

01:10:15.340 --> 01:10:17.810
The latest video about
why I hate Japanese TV.

01:10:17.810 --> 01:10:20.460
I had to learn how to use a blue screen

01:10:20.460 --> 01:10:22.340
in a way that I'd never done before,

01:10:22.340 --> 01:10:25.020
super impose myself into
a miniature dioRama.

01:10:25.020 --> 01:10:27.710
And I enjoyed that, every
video is a creative challenge.

01:10:27.710 --> 01:10:28.930
There's something to be learned,

01:10:28.930 --> 01:10:30.380
Something new to be discovered.

01:10:30.380 --> 01:10:33.530
I don't have the kind of personality

01:10:33.530 --> 01:10:34.983
to keep putting out regular content.

01:10:34.983 --> 01:10:39.330
- I think you do. I think you
have the personality for it.

01:10:39.330 --> 01:10:42.441
I think that maybe you just, you know--

01:10:42.441 --> 01:10:43.274
- But I don't know.

01:10:43.274 --> 01:10:48.100
I still enjoy kind of, the
filmmaking process really.

01:10:48.100 --> 01:10:48.933
Whereas you guys pull out,

01:10:48.933 --> 01:10:50.530
the kind of commentary aspect, right?

01:10:50.530 --> 01:10:51.487
Discussing, debating things,

01:10:51.487 --> 01:10:53.350
anime, there may you have so many topics?

01:10:53.350 --> 01:10:55.270
- I think for us, it's more just like

01:10:55.270 --> 01:10:57.440
we get on camera, we
make a fool of ourselves.

01:10:57.440 --> 01:10:59.390
Our audience likes it and laughs at it.

01:11:00.240 --> 01:11:03.010
- I felt like a huge weight was lifted,

01:11:03.010 --> 01:11:04.300
when I stopped editing my own videos.

01:11:04.300 --> 01:11:06.320
I felt like I actually had time at time,

01:11:06.320 --> 01:11:07.940
like time to start uploading more

01:11:07.940 --> 01:11:10.520
because, I'm uploading normally like,

