01:51:06.350 --> 01:51:09.600
because YouTube is not even
15 years old at this point,

01:51:09.600 --> 01:51:12.120
we don't know if it's
going to be the next TV

01:51:12.120 --> 01:51:13.773
or if it's just a fad.

01:51:14.700 --> 01:51:15.973
- Get ready for Tik ToK.

01:51:18.713 --> 01:51:19.930
- You never know, honestly.

01:51:19.930 --> 01:51:22.447
So like that's what's like
the scariest thing about it.

01:51:22.447 --> 01:51:25.230
And I think that's why I've slowly come

01:51:25.230 --> 01:51:28.050
to the realization that,
like, I can't put all my eggs

01:51:28.050 --> 01:51:30.790
into the YouTube basket,
because you really don't know,

01:51:30.790 --> 01:51:34.480
if it's one day it's
just going to disappear.

01:51:34.480 --> 01:51:36.070
- Do you have a plan or anything?

01:51:36.070 --> 01:51:38.530
If YouTube fell through,
wouldn't you have like a backup?

01:51:38.530 --> 01:51:40.890
- I guess for you would
be the film industry.

01:51:40.890 --> 01:51:42.490
- That will teach English again.

01:51:43.737 --> 01:51:45.260
Back to teaching Japanese students

01:51:45.260 --> 01:51:46.910
how to pronounce the word salmon.

01:51:48.082 --> 01:51:50.740
(speaks in foreign language)

01:51:50.740 --> 01:51:53.864
- How to say without
pen pronouncing the p.

01:51:53.864 --> 01:51:54.947
- This a pen.

01:51:59.060 --> 01:52:01.850
That's my backup plan.

01:52:01.850 --> 01:52:03.291
Yeah. It's just like.

01:52:03.291 --> 01:52:04.124
- I haven't thought about it.

01:52:04.124 --> 01:52:06.190
- I believe that it'll be all right.

01:52:06.190 --> 01:52:08.160
I'm seeing YouTube Weill will be okay.

01:52:08.160 --> 01:52:09.690
We talked about the turmoil of YouTube,

01:52:09.690 --> 01:52:10.753
how dodgy it is, but
at the end of the day,

01:52:10.753 --> 01:52:13.770
YouTube is fairly stable
and it is fairly okay.

01:52:13.770 --> 01:52:15.870
- It's more sustainable
than I think YouTubers

01:52:15.870 --> 01:52:17.348
like to make out of it.

01:52:17.348 --> 01:52:18.670
We like to play devil's advocate, a lot.

01:52:18.670 --> 01:52:21.660
- Definitely. And I've got
a podcast I do as well.

01:52:21.660 --> 01:52:23.355
Occasionally I do sponsor stuff.

01:52:23.355 --> 01:52:24.188
I've got Patreons.

01:52:24.188 --> 01:52:25.690
So I have lots of different avenues.

01:52:25.690 --> 01:52:29.420
If YouTube falls, then hopefully
I can bounce back somehow.

01:52:29.420 --> 01:52:30.940
Make my transition to the film industry.

01:52:30.940 --> 01:52:33.150
But that's why I do have my
eye always on the future.

01:52:33.150 --> 01:52:35.920
You get the film, moving to
TV, doing something like that.

01:52:35.920 --> 01:52:37.380
As a backup. But then the day really,

01:52:37.380 --> 01:52:40.670
YouTube will always be for
the foreseeable future,

01:52:40.670 --> 01:52:41.503
hopefully 10 years.

01:52:41.503 --> 01:52:43.000
That's my main bread and butter.

01:52:43.000 --> 01:52:43.833
That's what I do.

01:52:43.833 --> 01:52:45.040
That's what I enjoy doing.

01:52:45.040 --> 01:52:46.520
- The way I see it, as
well as like, you know,

01:52:46.520 --> 01:52:48.930
it's not often you get
an opportunity like this

01:52:48.930 --> 01:52:50.037
in your life.

01:52:50.037 --> 01:52:52.080
Even if it did fall
through so fucking what?

01:52:52.080 --> 01:52:53.303
I had a good time.

01:52:54.543 --> 01:52:56.290
- Even if you were like a millionaire,

01:52:56.290 --> 01:52:59.030
if you were worth $500 million Connor,

01:52:59.030 --> 01:53:00.753
you probably still do what you do.

01:53:02.013 --> 01:53:02.900
- It's what I'd love to do.

01:53:02.900 --> 01:53:03.870
- You don't do it for the money.

01:53:03.870 --> 01:53:05.730
You do it because you
actually enjoy commentating.

01:53:05.730 --> 01:53:07.750
You enjoy the creativity, enjoy the fun.

01:53:07.750 --> 01:53:09.590
And I'm sure that's like you guys, right?

01:53:09.590 --> 01:53:10.423
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:53:10.423 --> 01:53:12.060
- I love the creative aspects for it.

01:53:12.060 --> 01:53:14.900
Like I went through four
years of engineering

01:53:14.900 --> 01:53:18.013
to realize I don't want to do engineering.

01:53:18.013 --> 01:53:18.846
You know what I mean?

01:53:18.846 --> 01:53:20.890
- I feel like that's engineering.

01:53:20.890 --> 01:53:22.060
I did engineering, as well.

01:53:22.060 --> 01:53:24.440
You did three years of
engineering to realize,

01:53:24.440 --> 01:53:26.410
I think I like engineering, like I do.

01:53:26.410 --> 01:53:28.270
I remember, I distinctly, I remember this.

01:53:28.270 --> 01:53:30.330
I was in my dissertation,
my final dissertation

01:53:30.330 --> 01:53:32.030
and I did some electrical
engineering for it.

01:53:32.030 --> 01:53:34.530
And I was like, I think I
like electrical engineering

01:53:34.530 --> 01:53:35.457
more than--
(the rest laugh)

01:53:35.457 --> 01:53:37.413
And I was like, fuck.

01:53:38.720 --> 01:53:39.660
It's like, kind of like shit,

01:53:39.660 --> 01:53:41.530
I've been doing mechanical
for three years.

01:53:41.530 --> 01:53:45.764
- Did you have a diploma
or did not have a diploma?

01:53:45.764 --> 01:53:47.960
- I got a certificate from high school.

01:53:47.960 --> 01:53:49.440
- Special boys studies.

01:53:49.440 --> 01:53:53.310
Now I did business and
English linguistics.

01:53:53.310 --> 01:53:54.958
- How long did it take for that?

01:53:54.958 --> 01:53:57.420
- I guess that was three years.

01:53:57.420 --> 01:53:59.466
- And then straight to Japan?
- And then straight to Japan.

01:53:59.466 --> 01:54:01.080
But I felt like that is a litmus test.

01:54:01.080 --> 01:54:02.850
If you had like a billion dollars, right?

01:54:02.850 --> 01:54:05.144
You still will probably want
to do what you do now, right?

01:54:05.144 --> 01:54:06.360
You want to live in Japan.

01:54:06.360 --> 01:54:08.339
Experience the country,
upload so many videos,

01:54:08.339 --> 01:54:09.266
and see people.

01:54:09.266 --> 01:54:10.310
And that is the main thing, right?

01:54:10.310 --> 01:54:12.990
That's the main test,
If you enjoy it or not.

01:54:12.990 --> 01:54:14.390
- I've always wanted to and I think

01:54:14.390 --> 01:54:15.670
the other boys can attest to this,

01:54:15.670 --> 01:54:17.390
but like I've always wanted to ask you

01:54:17.390 --> 01:54:20.820
and I'm pretty sure I've to
ask you personally about,

01:54:20.820 --> 01:54:22.890
how you got to do your Ted.

01:54:22.890 --> 01:54:24.840
- I completely forgot about that.

01:54:24.840 --> 01:54:27.180
- I fucking love Ted talk, as a meme?

01:54:27.180 --> 01:54:28.454
- Yeah.

01:54:28.454 --> 01:54:29.940
- It's like, welcome to my Ted talk.

01:54:29.940 --> 01:54:31.300
Right. Like I love that part.

01:54:31.300 --> 01:54:34.570
So we want to know how you got that.

01:54:34.570 --> 01:54:35.640
How that opportunity came up?

01:54:35.640 --> 01:54:37.763
and like, just walk us
through the whole process.

01:54:39.722 --> 01:54:41.250
- Somebody said, "do you
want to do a Ted talk,

01:54:41.250 --> 01:54:43.962
about being a YouTuber in North Japan,

01:54:43.962 --> 01:54:45.219
and talk about what it's like."

01:54:45.219 --> 01:54:46.461
- Is that Tohoku University?

01:54:46.461 --> 01:54:50.280
- Yes, Tohoku University which is one of

01:54:50.280 --> 01:54:52.750
the top three university
in Japan actually.

01:54:52.750 --> 01:54:54.190
And they were like, do I do a Ted talk

01:54:54.190 --> 01:54:56.384
on how YouTube can be used as a way of

01:54:56.384 --> 01:54:58.010
encouraging people to come and talk?

01:54:58.010 --> 01:54:59.480
And I was like, yeah right.

01:54:59.480 --> 01:55:01.660
But I'm quite nervous at public speaking.

01:55:01.660 --> 01:55:04.283
Because it's quite scary
going you doing this,

01:55:04.283 --> 01:55:06.770
where it's three, four
drunkards in a room,

01:55:06.770 --> 01:55:10.531
just standing in front
of a Canon DSLR camera.

01:55:10.531 --> 01:55:12.980
To standing in front of 2000 people going,

01:55:12.980 --> 01:55:14.580
Excellent. Brilliant, wonderful.

01:55:18.780 --> 01:55:21.170
- Absolutely. It's because
like there's no feedback.

01:55:21.170 --> 01:55:22.003
You're just talking to a camera.

01:55:22.003 --> 01:55:23.990
- I fucking love making a fool of myself

01:55:23.990 --> 01:55:26.090
in front of people. I don't know.

01:55:26.090 --> 01:55:28.690
I think I'm too British and
I want to do a TED Talk.

01:55:29.680 --> 01:55:31.660
- I would like unironically say like,

01:55:31.660 --> 01:55:33.190
I would love to do a Ted Talk.

01:55:33.190 --> 01:55:34.023
- What about?

01:55:35.003 --> 01:55:36.260
Talk to Jelly.

01:55:36.260 --> 01:55:38.100
- Honestly, if someone was
like, can you do a Ted Talk

01:55:38.100 --> 01:55:40.530
about white animes or
something, I'm like, fuck yeah.

01:55:40.530 --> 01:55:42.626
- Exactly, it's gotta be
something you're passionate about.

01:55:42.626 --> 01:55:44.010
And then I'll speak to
him about how YouTube is

01:55:44.010 --> 01:55:45.530
a great tool for breaking North Japan.

01:55:45.530 --> 01:55:46.750
I had lots to say on the subject,

01:55:46.750 --> 01:55:49.100
given that I'd done it for
five years at that time.

01:55:49.100 --> 01:55:51.600
- I saw the video and you
seem like quite natural.

01:55:52.751 --> 01:55:54.980
- I was the last fucking
speaker of the day.

01:55:54.980 --> 01:55:56.280
And I was like, as I said,

01:55:56.280 --> 01:55:57.980
I'm nervous doing public speaking.

01:55:58.930 --> 01:56:00.230
And this is the biggest
speech I've ever done

01:56:00.230 --> 01:56:01.100
in front of a crowd.

01:56:01.100 --> 01:56:02.860
Was it like 2000 people or something,

01:56:02.860 --> 01:56:04.460
and everyone's talking
and I'm the last one,

01:56:04.460 --> 01:56:06.560
I'm just sitting there like, just shaking.

01:56:07.967 --> 01:56:10.770
And then you go up and you're
like, I need to act cool.

01:56:10.770 --> 01:56:14.393
And I'm like, yes. And there's
like a timer, before you.

01:56:17.081 --> 01:56:18.020
I've got like 15 minutes ago.

01:56:18.020 --> 01:56:21.573
14, 14:59, 14:58, 14:57,

01:56:22.657 --> 01:56:24.513
This seems good, isn't it?

01:56:24.513 --> 01:56:26.150
And the people love YouTube.

01:56:26.150 --> 01:56:26.983
- Was there a part of
you where you're like,

01:56:26.983 --> 01:56:28.307
should have timed this to one minute,

01:56:28.307 --> 01:56:30.330
and I went 10 seconds over.

01:56:30.330 --> 01:56:32.230
Oh my God, I've got to speed the rest.

01:56:32.230 --> 01:56:34.040
- I remember watching loads.

01:56:34.040 --> 01:56:35.710
I remember I watched Ken Robinson,

01:56:35.710 --> 01:56:37.010
which is a really good Ted Talk.

01:56:37.010 --> 01:56:39.780
Ken Robinson talked about
how schools kill creativity.

01:56:39.780 --> 01:56:41.330
It's when the best speeches on Ted.

01:56:41.330 --> 01:56:43.280
And one of the biggest, most
viewed, it's a great speech.

01:56:43.280 --> 01:56:45.030
And I just thought, I'll just do that.

01:56:45.030 --> 01:56:47.920
I watched Ken Robinson
speech, like 20 times.

01:56:47.920 --> 01:56:50.010
I'll just walk him, across stage

01:56:50.010 --> 01:56:53.047
and I'll deliver lines
like this, in intonation.

01:56:53.047 --> 01:56:57.310
And I remember I comment like
mimicking his intonation,

01:56:57.310 --> 01:57:00.043
I'm just like Ken Robinson and it worked.

01:57:00.043 --> 01:57:03.510
- Did you do like a little of prep?

01:57:03.510 --> 01:57:07.250
- I scripted the speech
and then in my apartment,

01:57:07.250 --> 01:57:08.310
the day before,

01:57:08.310 --> 01:57:10.543
I literally just did it
10 times, 10 times go.

01:57:10.543 --> 01:57:12.603
And I just did it once, I did it twice,

01:57:12.603 --> 01:57:13.670
like a tally chart

01:57:13.670 --> 01:57:16.030
and I was like five
times cross, six times.

01:57:16.030 --> 01:57:17.580
And that's all there is to it.

01:57:17.580 --> 01:57:19.130
And that wasn't too bad.

01:57:19.130 --> 01:57:20.923
Cause obviously you can just wing it.

01:57:21.822 --> 01:57:23.350
I did a Japanese speech contest once,

01:57:23.350 --> 01:57:27.082
and then that was extremely difficult,

01:57:27.082 --> 01:57:28.020
because it was in Japanese,

01:57:28.020 --> 01:57:30.680
and you're not allowed to
deviate from your own script.

01:57:30.680 --> 01:57:33.130
Cause the judges had your
script in front of you.

01:57:37.709 --> 01:57:39.370
So you have to go word by word, perfect.

01:57:39.370 --> 01:57:40.203
And so I'd done that.

01:57:40.203 --> 01:57:42.590
And that was obscenely complicated.

01:57:42.590 --> 01:57:43.423
- What was the process of that?

01:57:43.423 --> 01:57:44.960
Like how did you get into that?

01:57:44.960 --> 01:57:48.508
- I had to focus on like one
paragraph a day over a period.

01:57:48.508 --> 01:57:49.440
- How long was it?

01:57:49.440 --> 01:57:53.240
- It was a 15 minute speech
delivered entirely Japanese.

01:57:53.240 --> 01:57:55.980
I wrote it in English,
translated it and perfected it.

01:57:55.980 --> 01:57:58.280
And then I just broke
it down into paragraphs.

01:57:58.280 --> 01:58:00.660
Used a memorization
technique to get through it.

01:58:00.660 --> 01:58:03.113
- At that point, how good
was your Japanese speaking?

01:58:05.352 --> 01:58:06.848
Like conversational?
Lasting conversational?

01:58:06.848 --> 01:58:07.681
- I was conversational.

01:58:07.681 --> 01:58:10.110
I'd be learning Japanese
two years by that point.

01:58:10.110 --> 01:58:10.943
- Okay. Yeah.

01:58:10.943 --> 01:58:11.776
- And they gave me four,

01:58:11.776 --> 01:58:13.113
I'd done another speech
contest and I just forgot it.

01:58:13.113 --> 01:58:16.322
I got like three paragraphs.

01:58:16.322 --> 01:58:18.772
I was just like (speaks
in a foreign language).

01:58:18.772 --> 01:58:20.533
Fuck, I forgot it.

01:58:23.057 --> 01:58:23.950
I just bombed.

01:58:23.950 --> 01:58:25.744
So I had to read off a sheet
and it was really awkward.

01:58:25.744 --> 01:58:27.270
I was like, I've gotta
learn from that experience.

01:58:27.270 --> 01:58:28.283
- The next year, I'm gonna fuck

01:58:28.283 --> 01:58:29.910
this fucking speech contests.

01:58:29.910 --> 01:58:31.110
I'm going to do it.

01:58:31.110 --> 01:58:33.620
And it worked, I went all out.

01:58:33.620 --> 01:58:35.730
But so doing that really
prepared me for the Ted talk.

01:58:35.730 --> 01:58:37.900
And after that Ted talk, I was like, okay,

01:58:37.900 --> 01:58:39.887
I don't feel that nervous
about public speaking anymore.

01:58:39.887 --> 01:58:41.738
- Would you do another Ted
Talk, if you got the offer?

01:58:41.738 --> 01:58:42.837
- Sorry.

01:58:42.837 --> 01:58:43.670
- Would you do another Ted Talk?

01:58:43.670 --> 01:58:45.610
- Yeah, probably. I don't
know what I'll talk about.

01:58:45.610 --> 01:58:47.690
- About how awesome Japan is?

01:58:47.690 --> 01:58:48.970
- The value of Strong Zero?

01:58:48.970 --> 01:58:50.340
(the rest laugh)

01:58:50.340 --> 01:58:53.553
- Why the West needs Strong Zero?

01:58:54.457 --> 01:58:56.180
- Because that sounds
like my worst nightmare.

01:58:56.180 --> 01:58:59.070
I hate public speaking and
I've only gotten better

01:58:59.070 --> 01:59:01.490
about it because I've done panels,

01:59:01.490 --> 01:59:04.823
and it's like a fight or
flight kind of situation.

01:59:05.760 --> 01:59:06.760
- Once you've done it a few times,

01:59:06.760 --> 01:59:08.150
you do learn to enjoy it.

01:59:08.150 --> 01:59:08.983
- Yeah.

01:59:08.983 --> 01:59:10.660
- We just dragged him out recently,

01:59:10.660 --> 01:59:12.691
to do a video shooting in public,

01:59:12.691 --> 01:59:13.524
that you've never done before.

01:59:13.524 --> 01:59:15.420
- That was hell for me.

01:59:15.420 --> 01:59:16.253
- It's not out yet.

01:59:16.253 --> 01:59:19.090
It's actually going to be
out like in this weekend.

01:59:19.090 --> 01:59:21.063
- It's out by the time of this recording.

01:59:21.063 --> 01:59:22.400
- Basically we went around Akihabara,

01:59:22.400 --> 01:59:24.790
and buying as much stuff
as we could for 500 bucks.

01:59:24.790 --> 01:59:25.977
Yeah, this is all the stuff.

01:59:25.977 --> 01:59:27.390
That's the challenge.

01:59:27.390 --> 01:59:29.010
And we had a bunch of
challenges and he'd never done

01:59:29.010 --> 01:59:30.653
any video, anything like that before.

01:59:30.653 --> 01:59:33.230
And it was really fun seeing
him like, suffer slightly.

01:59:33.230 --> 01:59:34.470
- Were you guys like spotted in public,

01:59:34.470 --> 01:59:35.400
while you were filming.

01:59:35.400 --> 01:59:38.030
- Yeah. I mean, we got
permission to film where we did,

01:59:38.030 --> 01:59:40.180
but there were still like people there.

01:59:40.180 --> 01:59:41.590
But I think the biggest thing for me,

01:59:41.590 --> 01:59:44.650
is that I'm just like,
my videos have scripted.

01:59:44.650 --> 01:59:45.960
They're like perfectly planned,

01:59:45.960 --> 01:59:49.010
perfectly like I know
everything that's going to.

01:59:49.010 --> 01:59:51.137
So like when you just stick
a camera in front of me

01:59:51.137 --> 01:59:52.880
and just expect me to just talk.

01:59:52.880 --> 01:59:54.150
Unlike the podcast is different,

01:59:54.150 --> 01:59:56.790
because I got you guys,
it's like a conversation.

01:59:56.790 --> 01:59:58.591
I need that conversation.

01:59:58.591 --> 02:00:00.440
I need that bounce back.

02:00:00.440 --> 02:00:03.310
Because if it's just a camera,

02:00:03.310 --> 02:00:04.143
I kind of speak,

02:00:04.143 --> 02:00:06.850
and I almost like instinctively
wait for response.

02:00:06.850 --> 02:00:10.220
And then I realized that
no response is coming.

02:00:10.220 --> 02:00:11.640
So I started panicking.

02:00:11.640 --> 02:00:16.620
So I'm like, did I make the
points I wanted to make?

02:00:17.749 --> 02:00:21.190
And it's like, the first time
I tried filming the intro

02:00:21.190 --> 02:00:23.370
for this video, I was just like panicking.

02:00:23.370 --> 02:00:25.020
- People are looking at you?

02:00:25.020 --> 02:00:26.220
- Yeah.

02:00:26.220 --> 02:00:28.413
- I just wasn't used to it,

02:00:29.300 --> 02:00:31.680
but because, I remember my first panel,

02:00:31.680 --> 02:00:34.890
and I was equally as nervous
for my very first panel.

02:00:34.890 --> 02:00:37.380
And it was, back then, it was only

02:00:37.380 --> 02:00:39.221
in front of like 50 people or something.

02:00:39.221 --> 02:00:40.353
- Have you've been trying
to make convention?

02:00:40.353 --> 02:00:42.530
- I haven't, I got invited to a few,

02:00:42.530 --> 02:00:44.680
but I felt like I was misplaced.

02:00:44.680 --> 02:00:49.069
Cause I literally, if it's
about Satoshi Kon, we're in.

02:00:49.069 --> 02:00:50.497
(Garnt laughs)

02:00:50.497 --> 02:00:53.522
- I feel you'd be good
on the panel, like Garnt.

02:00:53.522 --> 02:00:54.890
- Maybe if it's about Japan stuff.

02:00:54.890 --> 02:00:55.810
Yeah, maybe. I don't know.

02:00:55.810 --> 02:00:56.960
- Yeah. I mean, if you're talking

02:00:56.960 --> 02:00:57.793
about something you know about,

